# The crypta KMS provider this repo's RELEASE build is overlaid with, pinned.
#
# sigil-enterprises/ducklake is a PUBLIC fork and carries only the abstract
# DuckLakeEncryptionProvider; the concrete crypta implementation is private and
# was deliberately removed here (ducklake@9e73bf28). What stayed behind is the
# `if(EXISTS "${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/crypta-provider")` guard in
# src/CMakeLists.txt and the DuckLakeRegisterKmsProvider() call in
# src/ducklake_extension.cpp. Nothing in either repository ever populated that
# directory, so every extension this fork has ever released registered no
# factory and refused every ATTACH ... ENCRYPTION_SOCKET (ducklake#33, #46).
#
# Release.yml overlays the provider from this pin before building, and then
# asserts it actually linked. Pinned, not floating: the provider compiles
# against DuckLakeEncryptionProvider, and a crypta that moves under us produces
# a build failure at best and a silently wrong link at worst.
#
# crypta@5404ce1d is the head of crypta's `adopt-reworked-encryption-interface`
# (sigil-enterprises/crypta#75), NOT a default-branch commit, and that is a
# deliberate exception to the paragraph above rather than an oversight.
#
# WHY. This branch changes the DuckLakeEncryptionProvider factory signature
# (`DatabaseInstance &` first, `idx_t` TTL) and renames `LakeId()` to
# `GetLakeId()`. crypta's default branch implements the OLD signature, so the
# previous pin does not compile against this branch at all - measured, not
# predicted, on run 33266818856:
#
#   src/include/crypta/ducklake_crypta.hpp:85: error: 'LakeId() const' marked
#     'override', but does not override
#   src/crypta-provider/crypta_provider_init.cpp:55: error: cannot convert
#     '<lambda(std::string, std::string, int64_t)>' to
#     'DuckLakeEncryptionProvider::Factory'
#   helper.hpp:66: error: invalid new-expression of abstract class type
#     'DuckLakeCryptaProvider'
#
# The pair is genuinely circular for the length of the two pull requests: this
# branch cannot build against crypta main, and crypta#75 cannot build against
# ducklake main. Both sides therefore carry a temporary BRANCH pin at the other
# - crypta's ducklake-provider/ducklake.pin points here for the same reason -
# and both say so in the file rather than hiding it in a commit message.
#
# RE-PIN THIS to the squash-merge sha the moment crypta#75 lands on crypta's
# default branch. A release built off a branch pin is a release whose provider
# source can be rewritten out from under it.
CRYPTA_REPO=sigil-enterprises/crypta
CRYPTA_REF=5404ce1d4473a2ab45bf8e9c188843c7012a0602
